<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Overrides com.android.internal.R.xml.haptic_feedback_customization (matched by name), which is an
  empty <haptic-feedback-constants/> in stock and donor framework-res.

  WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
  On a coin DC motor One UI short-circuits AOSP haptic feedback. services.jar,
  HapticFeedbackVibrationProvider.getVibrationForHapticFeedback(int):

      if (VibRune.SUPPORT_HAPTIC_FEEDBACK_ON_DC_MOTOR && VibratorHelper.sMotorType == 1) {
          if (effectId != 0 && effectId != 0xc3d4) index = -1;   // -> returns null
          ...
      }

  i.e. every constant except LONG_PRESS (0) and GRAB (0xc3d4) returns null, and
  VibratorManagerService logs "vibration absent for constant N" and ends IGNORED_UNSUPPORTED.
  (UN1CA's services.jar patch 9001 adds the power key long press to that list.)

  VibratorManagerService.performHapticFeedbackForInputDeviceInternal consults
  HapticFeedbackCustomization BEFORE that method, and falls back to it only when the SparseArray
  has no entry for the constant. So an entry here pre-empts the DC short-circuit entirely: the
  effect below is a plain AOSP Composed(StepSegment), SingleVibrationSession's
  `instanceof SemHapticSegment` test fails, and it goes down the stock VibrationThread ->
  SetAmplitudeVibratorStep -> VibratorController.on(durationMs) -> our IVibrator::on().
  An entry here does NOT follow the touch intensity slider; it is a fixed length.

  WHY waveform-effect AND NOT predefined-effect
  predefined-effect would call IVibrator::perform(), whose duration table lives inside the compiled
  HAL binary. waveform-effect puts the millisecond value here, in text. amplitude="default" is
  mandatory and is what makes this resolve on a HAL with capabilities == 0:
  StepSegment.areVibrationFeaturesSupported() only demands hasAmplitudeControl() when the amplitude
  is not DEFAULT_AMPLITUDE.

  WHAT IS LISTED, AND WHY SO LITTLE (1.2.2, owner's choice "stock base + fixes", 10-10)
  Stock T970 (One UI 5.1.1, its last firmware) left almost every UI haptic silent on this motor: only the DC click
  (touch slider, stock 45/55/70 ms), the PIN pad (50 ms) and long patterns were felt
  (ops/release122/haptic_audit/VIBRATION_AUDIT.md). 1.0-1.2.1 listed 15 constants at 90 ms here,
  so switches (50051), Google app taps (1), confirm/reject (16/17), context click (6), the status
  bar capsule (50104) and the AOSP gesture/toggle ids buzzed where stock was silent. Those are
  removed: they return to stock silence, and gestures, the taskbar, the navigation buttons and
  long presses are unaffected because they are DC clicks (SEP 50124), not these constants.
  Kept, all at or near the stock DC click length and above the ~40 ms perception floor
  (handoff/04 SS1.1):
    50025 EFFECT_CLICK   lock screen PIN pad and pattern dots. 50 ms = stock PIN pad length.
                         Pattern dots fire ~100 ms apart and this brakeless motor needs
                         110-150 ms to stop, so longer steps would smear.
    3     KEYBOARD_TAP   keyboards that use AOSP haptics (Gboard). Without an entry it is silent.
                         45 ms = stock default DC click (Samsung Keyboard uses the DC click).
    50057 EFFECT_HEAVY_CLICK_LONG_PRESS  long press on the quick panel brightness or volume bar.
                         Measured on the T970 (1.2.1, 10-10 19:09): 4 presses, 4
                         "external | ignored_unsupported", while every other long press is a DC
                         click. 45 ms like the default DC click (ops/release122/haptic_50057/).
  Left out on purpose: 50065 (slider ends, and text selection handles fire it per character,
  which this motor would smear into one buzz), 50050 and the scroll/tick ids (repeat within
  one gesture), 7/8 (key release would double every keypress).
-->
<haptic-feedback-constants>

    <constant id="50025">
        <vibration-effect>
            <waveform-effect><waveform-entry durationMs="50" amplitude="default"/></waveform-effect>
        </vibration-effect>
    </constant>

    <constant id="3">   <!-- KEYBOARD_TAP / KEYBOARD_PRESS -->
        <vibration-effect>
            <waveform-effect><waveform-entry durationMs="45" amplitude="default"/></waveform-effect>
        </vibration-effect>
    </constant>

    <constant id="50057">
        <vibration-effect>
            <waveform-effect><waveform-entry durationMs="45" amplitude="default"/></waveform-effect>
        </vibration-effect>
    </constant>

</haptic-feedback-constants>
